.TH ORA-NUM 1 "November 2026" "ora-num 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ora-num \- convert between Oracle internal NUMBER bytes and decimal numbers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ora-num
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR format ]
.I bytes
.br
.B ora-num
.B \-r
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR format ]
.I number
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ora-num
decodes an Oracle
.B NUMBER
from its internal base-100 byte representation, as shown by
.BR DUMP() ,
.BR "DUMP(x, 16)" ,
.B RAWTOHEX()
or a block dump, and prints its exact decimal value.
This is the default mode.
.PP
With
.BR \-r ,
the direction is reversed:
.I number
is a decimal number and
.B ora-num
prints its internal representation.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-reverse
Convert a decimal number into the Oracle internal representation.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " \fIformat\fR"
The input format, or with
.B \-r
the output format.
.I format
is one of:
.RS
.TP
.B auto
Detect the input format (see
.BR "INPUT FORMATS" ).
With
.BR \-r ,
the same as
.BR hex .
This is the default.
.TP
.B dec
Decimal bytes, e.g.\&
.BR 194,2,24 .
.TP
.B hex
Hexadecimal bytes, e.g.\&
.B C20218
or
.BR c2,2,18 .
.RE
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Also print the bytes, the sign, the base-100 exponent and the base-100
digits.
With
.BR \-r ,
the
.B DUMP()
form is printed as well.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a help summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH INPUT FORMATS
Without
.BR \-r ,
.I bytes
may be given in any of the following forms.
Quote it when it contains spaces.
.TP
.B DUMP(x)
.B "Typ=2 Len=3: 194,2,24"
.br
The
.B Typ=2 Len=\fIn\fB:
prefix is removed and the bytes are read as decimal.
.TP
.B DUMP(x, 16)
.B "Typ=2 Len=3: c2,2,18"
.br
The same prefix, with bytes in hexadecimal.
.TP
Decimal byte list
.B 194,2,24
or
.B "194 2 24"
.br
Bytes separated by commas or whitespace.
.TP
Contiguous hex
.B C20218
or
.B 0xc20218
.br
As returned by
.BR RAWTOHEX() .
Upper and lower case are accepted, and so is a
.B 0x
prefix.
.TP
Hex-dump style
.B "c2 02 18"
.br
Space-separated two-character hex pairs.
.TP
Partial block dump
.B "col 0: [ 3] c2 02 18"
.br
A column entry from
.BR "ALTER SYSTEM DUMP DATAFILE" :
.BR col ,
the column number, a colon, the byte count in brackets and the bytes in
hex.
The bytes are always read as hex, and their number must match the count.
A NULL column
.RB ( "col 1: *NULL*" )
is reported as an error.
.PP
With
.BR "\-f auto" ,
block dump input is read as hex.
Other input is read as hex if it is a single token, contains the letters
.BR a \- f ,
or consists of space-separated two-character pairs; otherwise it is read as
decimal.
Use
.B \-f dec
or
.B \-f hex
when the detection guesses wrong, e.g.\&
.B "62 99"
is read as hex unless
.B \-f dec
is given.
.SH REVERSE CONVERSION
With
.BR \-r ,
.I number
consists of an optional sign, digits with an optional decimal point
.RB ( .5
and
.B 5.
are accepted) and an optional exponent
.RB ( 1.5e\-7 ,
.BR 1E+3 ).
.B \(ti
and
.B \-\(ti
stand for positive and negative infinity; quote them so the shell does not
expand them.
Negative numbers can be given directly, without
.BR \-\- .
.PP
The result is printed as contiguous hex, or as a comma-separated decimal
byte list with
.BR "\-f dec" .
.PP
A NUMBER holds at most 20 base-100 digits, which is up to 40 significant
decimal digits.
Longer input is rounded half away from zero, as Oracle does, and a note
with the rounded value is printed to standard error.
.PP
Numbers with a magnitude of 1e126 or more, including numbers that reach it
by rounding, are rejected.
So are non-zero numbers with a magnitude below 1e\-130; Oracle stores those
as zero, but
.B ora-num
reports an error.
.SH ENCODING
The first byte holds the sign and the base-100 exponent; the following
bytes are base-100 digits, most significant first.
A NUMBER is at most 22 bytes long.
.TP
Zero
The single byte
.BR 0x80 .
.TP
Positive numbers
Exponent byte
.BR "\(>= 0x80" ,
exponent = byte \- 193, digit = byte \- 1.
.TP
Negative numbers
Exponent byte
.BR "< 0x80" ,
exponent = 62 \- byte, digit = 101 \- byte.
The digits are followed by a
.B 0x66
terminator, unless all 20 digits are used.
.TP
Infinity
.B "0xFF 0x65"
is positive infinity, printed as
.BR \(ti ;
the single byte
.B 0x00
is negative infinity, printed as
.BR \-\(ti .
.PP
The value is
.RS
sign \(mu \(*S digit[i] \(mu 100^(exponent \- i)
.RE
where the first digit has index 0.
.B 0x80
and
.B 0x00
followed by digits are the smallest (\-65) and largest (62) exponents, so
the representable magnitudes range from 1e\-130 to just below 1e126.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The conversion succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
The input could not be parsed or is not a valid NUMBER.
An error message is printed to standard error.
.TP
.B 2
The command line is invalid, e.g.\& a missing argument, an extra argument or
an invalid
.B \-f
value.
Because the input may start with a minus sign, an unknown option given
instead of the input is taken as the input and fails with status 1.
.SH EXAMPLES
Decode the output of
.BR DUMP() :
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ ora\-num "Typ=2 Len=5: 194,13,35,57,79"
1234.5678
.EE
.RE
.PP
Decode a hex string and show its components:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ ora\-num \-v C20218
bytes:    C2 02 18
sign:     +
exponent: 1 (base 100)
digits:   [1, 23]
value:    123
.EE
.RE
.PP
Decode a column from a block dump:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ ora\-num "col 0: [ 2] c1 04"
3
.EE
.RE
.PP
Encode a decimal number:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ ora\-num \-r \-1234.5678
3D59432D1766
$ ora\-num \-r \-f dec \-1234.5678
61,89,67,45,23,102
.EE
.RE
.PP
Encode a number that needs rounding:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ ora\-num \-r 0.66666666666666666666666666666666666666666666
note: rounded to 0.6666666666666666666666666666666666666667 (20 base\-100 digits)
C04343434343434343434343434343434343434344
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
Oracle Database SQL Language Reference:
.BR DUMP ,
.BR RAWTOHEX ,
.BR HEXTORAW ,
.B NUMBER
data type.
